## Claim
Text: "Joe Biden wants to ban fracking"
Claim author: The Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign
Claim date: October 27, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### 1. Did Joe Biden, during his 2020 presidential campaign, explicitly state that he wants to ban all hydraulic fracturing (fracking) in the United States?
Answer: No, Joe Biden did not explicitly state that he wants to ban all fracking; in fact, he directly contradicted that claim by saying in a Pittsburgh speech, “I am not banning fracking. Let me say that again: I am not banning fracking. No matter how many times Donald Trump lies about me.”

Source URL: https://www.uagetinvolved.org/content/joe-biden-i-am-not-banning-fracking

### 2. What specific policy regarding fracking did Joe Biden's 2020 campaign platform include, as written in official campaign materials or on his website?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, Joe Biden's 2020 campaign platform did not include a ban on fracking; instead, it specifically called for "requiring aggressive methane pollution limits for new and existing oil and gas operations" and supporting a stop to "all oil drilling or gas drilling on federal lands."

Source URL: https://www.uagetinvolved.org/content/joe-biden-i-am-not-banning-fracking

### 3. Did Joe Biden ever say he would ban fracking on both federal and private lands, or did he limit any proposed ban to federal lands only?
Answer: Basedon the search result, Joe Biden did not say he would ban fracking on both federal and private lands; he limited any proposed ban to federal lands only. The article states that Biden said, “I am not banning fracking,” and that his campaign clarified his earlier statement “no new fracking” meant he would “not allow new federal land-drilling leases.”

Source URL: https://energynow.com/2020/09/the-biden-flip-flop-u-s-presidential-hopeful-biden-now-says-he-would-not-ban-fracking/

### 4. Is there a recorded speech, interview, or debate from Joe Biden before October 27, 2020, where he says he wants to ban fracking?
Answer: Yes, there is a recorded instance before October 27, 2020: during a March 2020 Democratic primary debate, Joe Biden said, “No more – no new fracking.” However, his campaign later clarified that he meant he would not allow new drilling leases on federal lands, and in September 2020 he explicitly stated, “I am not banning fracking,” directly contradicting the claim.

Source URL: https://energynow.com/2020/09/the-biden-flip-flop-u-s-presidential-hopeful-biden-now-says-he-would-not-ban-fracking/

### 5. Did Joe Biden’s running mate, Kamala Harris, express support for a fracking ban during the 2020 campaign, and if so, did Biden endorse that position?
Answer: Based on the search result, Kamala Harris did not express support for a fracking ban during the 2020 campaign; instead, she explicitly stated during the vice presidential debate that "Joe Biden will not end fracking" and reiterated on Twitter that "Joe Biden will not ban fracking." Therefore, Biden did not endorse a ban, as Harris affirmed his position against banning fracking.

Source URL: https://www.api.org/news-policy-and-issues/blog/2020/10/09/joe-biden-will-not-ban-fracking

### 6. How did Joe Biden’s stance on fracking in 2020 differ from his earlier positions as Vice President (2009–2017) or as a Senator?
Answer: In 2020, Joe Biden called for an end to new fracking and drilling on federal lands, stating there would be “no new fracking” under his administration, which marked a shift from the Obama administration’s policy—where he served as Vice President—that had “supported fracking while unsuccessfully pursuing tighter regulation of the practice.” The search result does not provide details on his earlier positions as a Senator.

Source URL: https://freebeacon.com/elections/biden-promises-no-more-drilling-under-his-presidency/

### 7. What did the 2020 Democratic Party platform say about fracking, and did Joe Biden explicitly endorse that platform?
Answer: According to the search result, the 2020 Democratic Party platform "left off a fracking prohibition and cuts to subsidies favored by more progressive activists," meaning it did not call for a fracking ban. However, the source does not explicitly state whether Joe Biden endorsed that platform; it only notes that Biden has "steered clear of calls for a broader ban on hydraulic fracturing" while promising to halt new oil and gas permitting on federal lands.

Source URL: https://www.spglobal.com/commodityinsights/en/market-insights/latest-news/natural-gas/082520-republicans-warn-of-fracking-ban-oil-and-gas-jobs-loss-on-day-one-of-convention

### 8. Did Joe Biden’s campaign or transition team release a clarification or denial after the Trump campaign made the claim "Joe Biden wants to ban fracking"?
Answer: Yes, the search result indicates that Joe Biden directly denied the claim, stating "I am not banning fracking" in a speech, which effectively served as a clarification from his campaign. However, the source does not specify that a formal release from the campaign or transition team was issued separately from Biden's own statement.

Source URL: https://www.uagetinvolved.org/content/joe-biden-i-am-not-banning-fracking

## Final Judgement
The fact-check evidence consistently shows that Joe Biden explicitly denied wanting to ban fracking, stating "I am not banning fracking" in a Pittsburgh speech and elsewhere. His campaign clarified that his earlier "no new fracking" comment referred only to halting new drilling leases on federal lands, not a total ban. His running mate, Kamala Harris, also affirmed that "Joe Biden will not end fracking." The 2020 Democratic Party platform did not include a fracking ban. Thus, the claim that Joe Biden wants to ban fracking is directly contradicted by his own statements and policy positions.

